    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Data</b> is a collection of raw, unprocessed facts and figures that have no meaning on their own, whereas <b>information</b> is data that has been processed (organised, sorted or calculated) so that it conveys meaning and is useful. Example of data: the numbers 3, 0, 5, 2 written on survey forms for \"servings of fruit eaten this week\". Example of information: a report stating that most Form 2 students eat fewer than two servings of fruit a week.</p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> <b>Journals</b> (for example a refereed public-health journal): reliable and authoritative because articles are peer-reviewed, and current. <b>People</b> (students and canteen staff, through interviews or questionnaires): readily available and highly relevant as a primary source, although answers may be less reliable.</p><p><b>(b)(ii)</b> <b>Relevance/appropriateness:</b> the manager should select information that applies to the country and to the age group being studied; a study of adults in another region would not reflect Caribbean secondary students. <b>Amount of depth required:</b> she must decide how much detail is needed, for example whether students will be interviewed in depth, whether only a sample of schools is used, or whether an island-wide survey is required; this determines which sources are suitable.</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>Input:</b> data from the surveys, questionnaires and interviews is captured or entered into a computer, for example through an online form. <b>Processing:</b> the data is collated and analysed, for example sorted by age and school and averages calculated. <b>Storage:</b> the data and results are stored in a database for later retrieval and comparison with future studies. <b>Output:</b> the findings are presented to the Ministry and schools using charts, graphs and a report, and the recommendations (such as healthier canteen menus) are disseminated. <b>Feedback:</b> the effect of the recommendations is evaluated in a follow-up survey, and the results loop back to the input stage to update the findings.</p>",
