    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Users provide input:</b> they enter the data (via keyboards, scanners and other devices) on which the system depends, so the accuracy of their input determines the quality of the information produced. <b>Users receive and interpret output:</b> they read reports and screens and use them to make decisions; without a user to interpret the output, the information has no value. (Also: users monitor the system to detect problems and provide feedback.)</p><p><b>(b)</b> HCI is the part of a computer system (software and hardware) that defines how a user interacts with, and receives responses from, the computer.</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>Command line:</b> the user types instructions (commands) at a prompt in a console; there are no menus or icons to choose from, so commands must be remembered. <b>Menu-driven:</b> the system displays a list of options, similar to a restaurant menu, and the user selects one to activate a process, as on an ATM. <b>Graphical user interface (GUI):</b> uses windows, icons, menus and a pointer (mouse or touch) so the user communicates by pointing and clicking on graphics.</p><p><b>(d)(i)</b> A <b>command line</b> interface. It gives faster, direct access to each server's operating system, uses less memory and processing than a GUI, and commands can be combined in a batch file or script to run the same fix across several servers.</p><p><b>(d)(ii)</b> A <b>virtual private network (VPN)</b>. It creates a secure, encrypted connection (tunnel) over the Internet to the organisation's network, so Harry can log in from home as if he were on the internal LAN.</p>",
