    "model": "<p><b>(a)(i)</b></p><figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 220\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Star topology: a switch in the centre with five PCs and a printer each connected by its own Ethernet cable.\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\"><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"110\"/><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"110\"/><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"30\"/><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"30\"/></g><rect x=\"130\" y=\"96\" width=\"60\" height=\"28\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#F5C542\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"114\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1a1a1a\">Switch</text><g fill=\"rgba(255,255,255,.06)\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"><rect x=\"235\" y=\"98\" width=\"50\" height=\"24\"/><rect x=\"185\" y=\"178\" width=\"50\" height=\"24\"/><rect x=\"85\" y=\"178\" width=\"50\" height=\"24\"/><rect x=\"35\" y=\"98\" width=\"50\" height=\"24\"/><rect x=\"85\" y=\"18\" width=\"50\" height=\"24\"/></g><rect x=\"185\" y=\"18\" width=\"50\" height=\"24\" fill=\"rgba(255,255,255,.06)\" stroke=\"#4FD18B\"/><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"260\" y=\"114\">PC 1</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"194\">PC 2</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"194\">PC 3</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"114\">PC 4</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"34\">PC 5</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"34\">Printer</text></g></svg><figcaption>Star topology for the HR department: each device has its own cable to the central switch.</figcaption></figure><p>[Diagram described] A <b>switch</b> (or hub) is drawn in the centre and labelled. Six separate Ethernet cables run from it to PC 1, PC 2, PC 3, PC 4, PC 5 and the <b>printer</b>, each labelled. No device is connected to any other device except through the switch.</p><p><b>(a)(ii)</b> This is a <b>star topology</b>. All data passes through the central switch, and each computer has its own cable to it, so the network does not depend on connections between computers: if PC 3 or its cable fails, only PC 3 is cut off and the others continue to communicate and print.</p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> <b>Encryption</b> is the coding or scrambling of data into an unreadable form (ciphertext) so that it can only be decoded and read by someone who has the correct decryption key; it is used, for example, to protect payroll files sent by e-mail.</p><p><b>(b)(ii)</b> <b>Authentication</b> is the process of confirming that a user, device or account holder is who they claim to be before access is allowed, by comparing what they enter (username and password, PIN, security key or fingerprint) with the details stored in a database.</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>Physical access control</b> uses tangible measures or devices to stop unauthorised people from reaching the computer equipment itself, for example a swipe card on the HR office door or a locked server room. <b>Logical access control</b> uses software, procedures and security principles (limited privileges, segregation of duties) to restrict what a person can do once at the computer, for example a user ID and password or file permissions so only HR staff can open salary records.</p>",
