    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> An <b>analogue</b> signal varies continuously and can take any value within a range, such as the voice signal on the school's VHF marine radio or the needle on a barometer. A <b>digital</b> signal has discrete values, represented as 0s and 1s (on/off), such as the GPS position displayed on the boat's screen or the student records on the office computer.</p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> 67 = 64 + 2 + 1, so 'C' = <b>01000011</b>. <b>(ii)</b> Morse code represents each letter with only two signal elements (dots and dashes), so SOS can be sent by flashing a torch, sounding a horn or tapping on a radio when a voice cannot be heard clearly; the short, internationally recognised pattern is easy to recognise even when the signal is weak or noisy.</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>Special-purpose notation:</b> scientific or mathematical notation, for example a formula for calculating the boat's speed in knots from distance and time. <b>Graphical representation:</b> a line graph of tide height against time of day, which shows instructors when the water in Road Harbour is deep enough to sail.</p><p><b>(d)</b> <b>Mailing list (e-mail):</b> advantage: one message reaches all parents in Tortola, Virgin Gorda and Anegada at once at almost no cost; disadvantage: messages may go to spam or be ignored. <b>Web-conferencing tool (e.g. Zoom or Teams):</b> advantage: instructors can hold a live parents' meeting across three islands without travel; disadvantage: it needs a stable, fast Internet connection, which may fail in bad weather. <b>FTP (file upload/download):</b> advantage: transfers very large race video files to the sponsor's server, which e-mail attachments cannot handle; disadvantage: plain FTP sends data unencrypted and requires the sponsor to set up server accounts.</p>",
    "teacherTip": "<p>Common errors: describing analogue as \"old\" and digital as \"new\"; binary of 67 written with 7 bits only (accept, but CAPE asks for 8 bits here) or with the bits reversed; calling a pie chart a \"special-purpose notation\"; tools without a reason, or advantages that are not linked to the school (distance between islands, file size). \"Justify\" requires a reason for every choice.</p>",
