    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> A <b>TPS</b> records and processes the routine, day-to-day transactions of the business. At a Sunrise Foods checkout the <b>input</b> is the scanned barcode of each item, the quantity and the customer's payment. The TPS looks up prices, totals the sale and reduces the stock level. Its <b>output</b> is a printed receipt and processed transactions (updated stock and sales records) that feed other systems.</p><p><b>(b)</b> An <b>MIS</b> takes data from the TPS and produces routine, scheduled reports for middle (tactical) managers to monitor and control operations, for example a weekly report comparing sales in the Kingston, Spanish Town and Mandeville stores. A <b>DSS</b> provides interactive models and data analysis to support semi-structured or unstructured decisions, allowing \"what-if\" questions; for example, the board can enter estimated rents, staffing and population figures to compare the likely profit of a store in Montego Bay with one in Ocho Rios.</p><p><b>(c)(i)</b> A <b>summarised report</b> condenses detailed data into totals or key figures, giving an overview rather than every transaction; for example, total sales for each department in each store for the week. <b>(ii)</b> An <b>exception report</b> lists only the items that fall outside normal limits so managers can act on them; for example, products whose stock has fallen below the reorder level, or a store whose daily takings are much lower than usual.</p><p><b>(d)</b> At the <b>operational</b> level, supervisors need detailed, internal, current information to make <b>structured</b> decisions that are routine and follow clear rules, for example reordering bread when stock falls below 40 loaves. At the <b>strategic</b> level, the board needs summarised information, often from external sources (population, competitors, tourism trends), covering long periods and the future, to make <b>unstructured</b> decisions that have no set procedure and need judgement, such as where to open a fourth store.</p>",
