    "model": "<p><b>(a)(i)</b> Layer 1 Physical, Layer 2 Data Link, Layer 3 Network, Layer 4 Transport, Layer 5 Session, Layer 6 Presentation, Layer 7 Application.</p><p><b>(a)(ii)</b> <b>HTTP</b> operates at the <b>Application</b> layer; it requests and transfers web pages between browser and web server. <b>TCP</b> operates at the <b>Transport</b> layer; it breaks data into segments and provides reliable, ordered delivery by acknowledging and resending lost segments. <b>IP</b> operates at the <b>Internet</b> layer; it gives each packet source and destination IP addresses and routes it across networks.</p><p><b>(b)</b> <b>Step 1 – physical connections and status lights:</b> check that the router and modem are powered and that the Internet/WAN light is on; if it is red or off, the fault is with the telephone line or modem, not the laptop, so restart them. <b>Step 2 – ipconfig:</b> run ipconfig on the laptop to view its IP address and default gateway; an address beginning 169.254 means the laptop did not receive an address from the router, so the router's DHCP should be checked or restarted. <b>Step 3 – ping:</b> ping the gateway (e.g. 192.168.1.1) and then a public IP address such as 8.8.8.8; replies from the gateway but not the public address show the LAN works but the Internet link is down. <b>Step 4 – nslookup:</b> run nslookup on a domain name; if the IP address pings but the name cannot be resolved, the DNS server setting is wrong and should be changed. (A speed test or checking firewall settings that block the laptop are also valid steps.)</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>Replace WEP with WPA3 (or WPA2) using AES encryption:</b> WEP can be cracked in minutes, whereas WPA3 with AES encrypts traffic so eavesdroppers cannot read it. <b>Set a long, strong network key (passphrase):</b> mixed case, numbers and symbols make it hard to guess. <b>Change the default router password:</b> otherwise anyone who knows the factory default could log in and change settings. <b>Enable MAC filtering on the staff network and use a separate guest SSID:</b> only listed device MAC addresses can join the staff network, and guests are kept apart from the owner's booking PC.</p>",
