    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> The <b>Waterfall</b> model is a linear, sequential approach: each stage (analysis, design, development, testing, implementation) is completed and signed off before the next begins, and requirements are fixed early. It is easy to manage and well documented but cannot easily accommodate changes. <b>Agile</b> development is iterative and incremental: the team delivers working parts of the system in short cycles (sprints), and the customer reviews each one, so requirements can change during development. <b>Recommendation: Agile.</b> The owner is unsure which features customers want and expects requirements to change before the cruise season; Agile lets a basic booking feature go live early to stop double bookings, then adds features based on feedback within the six months.</p><p><b>(b)</b> <b>Feasibility study:</b> deliverable – a <b>system proposal</b> (feasibility report) recommending whether the project is technically, economically and operationally worth doing. <b>Design:</b> deliverable – <b>diagrams and charts</b> (DFDs, screen and database designs) and a design specification. <b>Implementation:</b> deliverable – a <b>conversion plan</b> (e.g. parallel running of the ledger and the new system) and <b>documentation</b> such as the user manual and technical manual. (Others: project plan from planning/analysis; test plans from development.)</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>Questionnaires:</b> sent to regular passengers and travel agents. Advantage: they gather the views of many customers on St. Vincent and the other islands quickly and cheaply, which helps decide which booking features are needed. <b>Interviews:</b> with the owner and booking clerks. Advantage: the analyst can ask follow-up questions to understand in depth how bookings are taken and why double bookings happen. (Observation of clerks taking phone bookings or reviewing the ledger are also valid.)</p>",
