    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> Algorithms can be represented as (1) <b>natural language</b> (numbered steps in plain English), (2) <b>flowcharts</b> and (3) <b>pseudocode</b>. Pseudocode is more precise and less ambiguous than natural language because it uses a limited set of structured keywords (INPUT, IF, WHILE, OUTPUT) and indentation, so it can be translated directly into program code.</p><p><b>(b)</b></p><pre>START\n  children = 0\n  adults = 0\n  total = 0\n  INPUT age\n  WHILE age &lt;&gt; -1 DO\n     IF age &lt; 12 THEN\n        children = children + 1\n        total = total + 5\n     ELSE\n        adults = adults + 1\n        total = total + 10\n     ENDIF\n     INPUT age\n  ENDWHILE\n  OUTPUT \"Children: \", children\n  OUTPUT \"Adults: \", adults\n  OUTPUT \"Total fare: $\", total\nSTOP</pre><p><b>(c)</b> An algorithm is the <b>precursor</b> to a program: it is the step-by-step plan for solving the problem, designed and checked (for example with a trace table) before any code is written. A program is the algorithm translated into a programming language that a computer can execute. The stages of program development are: define and analyse the problem, design the algorithm, code the program, test and debug it, document it and maintain it, so the algorithm is produced at the design stage and drives the coding stage. Programs are written within a <b>programming paradigm</b>; for example, the <b>object-oriented</b> paradigm organises programs as objects with data and methods, and <b>Java</b> or Python are object-oriented languages (procedural: Pascal or C).</p>",
