    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> A <b>primary source</b> provides original, first-hand information collected by someone who experienced or observed the event, for example Keisha's interview with her grandmother about East End during Irma (or residents' own posts with photos). A <b>secondary source</b> interprets, summarises or analyses primary information produced by others, for example the Wikipedia article on Hurricane Irma or the journal article reviewing Caribbean recovery.</p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> <b>Wiki – advantage:</b> it is free, easy to search and broad, and its references can lead Keisha to other sources. <b>Disadvantage:</b> anyone can edit it, so content may be inaccurate or changed at any time and is not refereed. <b>(ii)</b> <b>Social media – advantage:</b> the Facebook group gives current, first-hand accounts and photographs from many residents. <b>Disadvantage:</b> posts are not verified, may be emotional or biased and can spread rumours, so they are unreliable as evidence.</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>Reliability:</b> the journal article is refereed (peer-reviewed by experts), so it is highly reliable. <b>Amount of detail (depth):</b> it provides detailed analysis with data and references rather than a brief overview. <b>Cost/availability:</b> it may sit behind a subscription, but Keisha may access it through the university library's electronic databases.</p><p><b>(d)</b> <b>Bias:</b> the blog presents one resident's personal opinions and may reflect only his area's experience or a grievance against the government, so Keisha should recognise it as one viewpoint and balance it with other sources. <b>Refereed versus un-refereed (accuracy):</b> the blog is un-refereed, so no expert has checked its facts; Keisha should cross-check figures such as the number of homes repaired against the government report before accepting them. (Currency and cultural context are also acceptable.)</p>",
    "teacherTip": "<p>Common errors: calling any Internet source \"secondary\" (a resident's eyewitness post is primary); disadvantages such as \"it is on the Internet\"; mixing up <b>characteristics</b> of sources (SO 1.5: availability, cost, currency, depth, breadth, reliability, format and medium) with <b>criteria</b> for selection (SO 1.6: bias, accuracy, cultural context, completeness, currency, refereed); naming a criterion without applying it to the blog.</p>",
