    "stimulus": "<p class=\"passage\"><b>Harbour Gems Jewellers</b> in George Town records every sale on a carbon-copy receipt book and keeps stock on index cards. Each Friday the owner, Mrs. Richardson, adds up the week's sales by hand and asks staff what customers have been saying. She is moving to a computerised point-of-sale (POS) system with a barcode scanner and a stock database before the next cruise season.</p>",
    "prompt": "<ol type=\"a\"><li>Distinguish between <b>qualitative</b> and <b>quantitative</b> data, giving <b>ONE</b> example of EACH from the jewellery shop. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Explain how <b>knowledge</b> differs from <b>information</b>, using a decision Mrs. Richardson might make. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Explain <b>THREE</b> characteristics of information that determine its value to Mrs. Richardson. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Compare manual and automated processing in the shop at <b>THREE</b> stages of the information processing cycle. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li>Explain the role of <b>feedback</b> in the information processing cycle. <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>",
    "frame": "<p><b>(a)</b> Qualitative data ... e.g. ... Quantitative data ... e.g. ...<br><b>(b)</b> Information is ... Knowledge is ... e.g. ...<br><b>(c)</b> Timeliness ... Accuracy/reliability ... Relevance/completeness ...<br><b>(d)</b> Input: manually ... automated ... Processing: ... Storage/retrieval: ...<br><b>(e)</b> Feedback ...</p>",
