    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Qualitative</b> data is opinion-based and subjective, describing qualities, for example customers commenting that the new silver bracelets look \"elegant but overpriced\". <b>Quantitative</b> data is measurement-based and objective, for example \"14 gold rings sold on Tuesday\" or the price of each item.</p><p><b>(b)</b> <b>Information</b> is data that has been processed so it has meaning, for example a report showing that ring sales double on days when three or more cruise ships are in port. <b>Knowledge</b> is the understanding gained from information, combined with experience, and its use in decision making: Mrs. Richardson knows from that report and past seasons that she should increase ring stock and schedule extra staff before the cruise calendar's busiest weeks.</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>Timeliness:</b> information is valuable only if it arrives in time to act; Friday hand totals are too late to restock popular items for Wednesday's ships. <b>Accuracy/reliability:</b> errors when adding receipts by hand give a false picture of sales, which can lead to over-ordering expensive stock. <b>Completeness:</b> if some receipt copies are missing, the totals are incomplete and decisions are based on part of the picture.</p><p><b>(d)</b> <b>Input:</b> manually, the sales clerk handwrites each sale on a receipt book, which is slow and error-prone; with automation, the barcode scanner captures the item code and price instantly and accurately. <b>Processing:</b> manually, Mrs. Richardson collates and adds the week's receipts by hand; automated, the POS calculates totals, sorts sales by item and updates stock automatically after each sale. <b>Storage and retrieval:</b> manually, stock cards and receipt books are filed in a cabinet and searching takes time and they may be lost or damaged; automated, data is stored in a database that can be searched in seconds and backed up off-site.</p><p><b>(e)</b> <b>Feedback</b> is the evaluation of output (for example, a stock report that does not match a physical count), which loops back to the input stage so that data can be corrected or updated.</p>",
