    "stimulus": "<p class=\"passage\">The <b>Seaside Community Health Centre</b> in Willemstad has installed a self-service check-in kiosk with a touch screen. The kiosk runs an operating system and a check-in application; a separate embedded system controls the queue-number display. Many patients are elderly, some are visually impaired, and visitors speak English, Dutch, Spanish or Papiamentu. Patients report that the buttons are small, the grey text on a white background is hard to read, the \"Back\" button moves position on each screen and there is no way to return to the start.</p>",
    "prompt": "<ol type=\"a\"><li>Distinguish between <b>application software</b> and an <b>embedded system</b>, using examples from the kiosk. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Explain the roles of an <b>IT professional</b> and of an <b>end user</b> in the kiosk system, and distinguish between <b>expert</b> and <b>novice</b> users. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Describe <b>THREE</b> ways the kiosk interface could be adapted to the characteristics of its users. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Evaluate the current kiosk interface using <b>FOUR</b> HCI principles. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>",
    "frame": "<p><b>(a)</b> Application software ... e.g. ... whereas an embedded system ... e.g. ...<br><b>(b)</b> The IT professional ... The end user ... An expert user ... whereas a novice ...<br><b>(c)</b> Age: ... Disability: ... Culture/language: ...<br><b>(d)</b> Ease of use: ... Ease of navigation: ... Alignment: ... Use of colour: ...</p>",
