    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Application software</b> is a program designed to carry out a specific task for the user; the check-in application lets patients enter their name or date of birth and confirm their appointment. An <b>embedded system</b> is a dedicated computer system built into a device to monitor or control one function; the controller inside the queue-number display receives the next number and shows it on the screen. The user does not install or run it as a separate program, and it cannot be used for other tasks.</p><p><b>(b)</b> The <b>IT professional</b> (for example the health centre's technician or the software supplier) installs, configures, maintains and secures the kiosk and fixes faults. The <b>end user</b> (patients and reception staff) uses the system to complete tasks such as checking in and does not need technical knowledge. An <b>expert user</b> is experienced and confident, uses the system often and may use shortcuts (a receptionist overriding a check-in), whereas a <b>novice user</b> has little experience, such as a first-time patient, and needs clear prompts, simple steps and help.</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>Age:</b> larger buttons and text, with fewer options per screen, for elderly patients with reduced eyesight or dexterity. <b>Disability:</b> a non-visual interface for visually impaired users, such as speech output through a headphone socket and tactile or Braille keys, plus a high-contrast mode. <b>Cultural differences:</b> a language choice (English, Dutch, Spanish, Papiamentu) on the first screen and universally understood icons.</p><p><b>(d)</b> <b>Ease of use:</b> poor, because the small buttons are hard to press accurately, especially for elderly patients. <b>Ease of navigation:</b> poor, because there is no way to return to the start, so a patient who makes a mistake is stuck. <b>Alignment:</b> poor, because the Back button moves position on each screen, so users cannot predict where controls are; it should be fixed in the same place. <b>Use of colour:</b> poor, because grey text on white has low contrast and is hard to read; dark text on a light background with a strong accent colour for buttons would be better. Overall the interface is not suitable for its users and should be redesigned.</p>",
    "teacherTip": "<p>Common errors: calling the operating system \"application software\"; describing an embedded system as \"software inside the computer\"; saying novices are \"not smart\"; adaptations not linked to a characteristic; \"evaluate\" answers that only describe. Each evaluation point should name the principle, give the evidence and make a judgement.</p>",
