    "stimulus": "<p class=\"passage\">A weather club at a school in Dominica records daily rainfall (mm) at Canefield. Its algorithm is shown below. The value −1 ends the input.</p><pre>total = 0\ndays = 0\nwet = 0\nINPUT rain\nWHILE rain &lt;&gt; -1 DO\n   total = total + rain\n   days = days + 1\n   IF rain &gt; 10 THEN\n      wet = wet + 1\n   ENDIF\n   INPUT rain\nENDWHILE\navg = total / days\nOUTPUT days, wet, avg</pre>",
    "prompt": "<ol type=\"a\"><li>Explain <b>THREE</b> characteristics of a well-designed algorithm. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Complete a trace table for the algorithm using the inputs 4, 12, 0, 25, 9, −1, and state the output. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li>Develop a pseudocode algorithm, using a <b>FOR</b> loop, that reads the marks of the 25 students in a class, outputs \"Pass\" or \"Fail\" for each student (the pass mark is 50), and finally outputs the highest mark. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li>Distinguish between <b>natural language</b> and a <b>flowchart</b> as ways of representing an algorithm. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>",
    "frame": "<p><b>(a)</b> Finite: ... Unambiguous/clearly defined: ... Flow of control: ... (General solution: ...)<br><b>(b)</b> rain | total | days | wet  ... Output: ...<br><b>(c)</b> START / highest = 0 / FOR count = 1 TO 25 / INPUT mark / IF mark &gt;= 50 THEN ... ELSE ... ENDIF / IF mark &gt; highest THEN ... / ENDFOR / OUTPUT highest / STOP<br><b>(d)</b> Natural language ... whereas a flowchart ...</p>",
