    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Finite number of steps:</b> the algorithm must end after a finite number of steps; here the loop stops when −1 is entered. <b>Clearly defined and unambiguous:</b> each step has only one meaning, so any person or computer following it produces the same result. <b>Flow of control:</b> control passes logically from one process to the next in a defined order, with clear entry to and exit from each loop or decision. (Also: it is a <b>general solution</b> that works for any valid set of inputs, not just one example.)</p><p><b>(b)</b></p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>rain</th><th>total</th><th>days</th><th>wet</th><th>avg</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>–</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>4</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>16</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>16</td><td>3</td><td>1</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>41</td><td>4</td><td>2</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>50</td><td>5</td><td>2</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>−1</td><td>50</td><td>5</td><td>2</td><td>10</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Output: <b>5, 2, 10</b> (5 days recorded, 2 wet days over 10 mm, average 10 mm).</p><p><b>(c)</b></p><pre>START\n  highest = 0\n  FOR count = 1 TO 25 DO\n     INPUT mark\n     IF mark &gt;= 50 THEN\n        OUTPUT \"Pass\"\n     ELSE\n        OUTPUT \"Fail\"\n     ENDIF\n     IF mark &gt; highest THEN\n        highest = mark\n     ENDIF\n  ENDFOR\n  OUTPUT \"Highest mark: \", highest\nSTOP</pre><p><b>(d)</b> <b>Natural language</b> expresses the steps in ordinary written sentences, which are easy for anyone to read but can be ambiguous and lengthy. A <b>flowchart</b> represents the steps graphically using standard symbols (ovals, rectangles, diamonds, parallelograms) joined by arrows, so the flow of control, decisions and loops can be seen at a glance.</p>",
    "teacherTip": "<p>Common errors: counting 10 as a wet day (the test is &gt; 10, not &gt;= 10); processing −1 as a reading; omitting the initial row of the trace table; using a WHILE loop when a FOR loop is asked for; initialising highest inside the loop; placing OUTPUT highest inside the loop; one IF statement doing both jobs so the highest mark is only updated for passes. In (a), \"it should be easy\" is not a syllabus characteristic.</p>",
