    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> The main reason I researched this problem is that the Seaside Pharmacy in Gros Islet was losing sales and putting patients at risk because of poor stock control. Fast-moving medicines such as insulin and children's antibiotics regularly ran out, so customers were sent to other pharmacies, while slow-moving items expired on the shelves. The owner estimated that about EC$4,900 of expired stock was written off in one quarter. Because the problem affected both the pharmacy's income and patients' access to essential medication, it was worth investigating for an IT-based solution.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One cause of the problem is that stock is recorded manually on paper stock cards that are updated only at closing time. Sales made during the day are not deducted until the evening, and on busy days some sales are never recorded, so the quantity on the card is higher than what is actually on the shelf. As a result, the pharmacist reorders too late and items run out.</p>\n<p>A second cause is that there is no system for tracking expiry dates or reorder levels. Staff do not apply \"first expiry, first out\" because the cards do not show expiry dates, and no one is alerted when an item falls below its minimum level. This leads to older stock being left at the back of the shelf until it expires, while popular items are not reordered in time.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> The pharmacists and counter staff at Seaside Pharmacy cannot tell accurately how much of each medicine is in stock or when it will expire, because stock is recorded by hand on cards that are updated only once a day. If this is not corrected, the pharmacy will continue to lose sales to competitors, write off expired medicine and leave patients without essential medication.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> <b>Guideline 1: Obtain permission and inform the staff.</b> I should get written permission from the owner before observing and tell the staff when and why I will be there. This is ethical and prevents staff from feeling they are being spied on.</p>\n<p><b>Guideline 2: Plan the objectives and use a checklist.</b> Before observing, I should decide exactly what to look for, such as how deliveries are checked in and when stock cards are updated, and prepare a checklist or record form. This keeps the observation focused and makes the notes easy to analyse.</p>\n<p><b>Guideline 3: Observe at different times, including peak periods.</b> I should observe on a quiet weekday morning and on a busy Saturday and delivery day. This shows how the system works under pressure, when most recording errors happen, rather than only on an easy day.</p>\n<p><b>Guideline 4: Be unobtrusive and keep information confidential.</b> I should stand where I do not interfere with serving customers and should not interrupt staff during sales. Any patient or prescription details I see must be kept confidential and left out of my notes, because pharmacy records are private health information.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> I would use a structured interview with the three staff members who receive deliveries and update the stock cards. Observation showed me what they do, but not why they skip updating the cards on busy days or how they decide when to reorder. In an interview I can ask them to explain each step, ask probing follow-up questions and clarify anything I did not understand. This enhances the research by filling the gaps left by observation and confirming whether what I saw is normal practice.</p>",
    "teacherTip": "<p><b>How marks are lost.</b> In (a), candidates restate the problem instead of the REASON for researching it. In (c), a problem statement without its three parts (who, how, what if not fixed) earns 1 mark. In (d), \"outline\" needs a guideline plus a short reason; four bare phrases earn only 4 of 8. Repeating one guideline in different words (\"be quiet\", \"do not disturb\") counts once. In (e), a questionnaire is a weak choice for a small group; an interview lets you probe.</p>",
