    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Solution 1: A custom-built stock database.</b> A local developer would build a Microsoft Access database with tables for products, suppliers and deliveries. Staff would enter sales and deliveries during the day, and the database would produce a reorder report and an expiry report each morning.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2: A commercial off-the-shelf pharmacy point-of-sale (POS) and inventory package.</b> The pharmacy would purchase a pharmacy POS package with barcode scanners. Each sale scanned at the counter is deducted from stock immediately, expiry dates are stored for each batch, and the system alerts staff when items fall below reorder level.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3: A cloud-based inventory subscription (SaaS).</b> The pharmacy would subscribe to an online inventory service accessed through a web browser on a tablet and the counter PC. Stock levels are stored online, and the service emails the pharmacist and suppliers when items need to be reordered.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Criterion 1: Technical feasibility.</b> This asks whether the hardware, software and technical skills needed for a solution are available. For example, the cloud solution needs a reliable Internet connection, while the POS package needs barcode scanners and a counter PC. A solution that the pharmacy cannot technically support should be rejected.</p>\n<p><b>Criterion 2: Economic feasibility.</b> This compares the cost of a solution (purchase, licences, hardware, training, maintenance) with the benefits it brings, such as fewer expired items written off and fewer lost sales. A solution is economically feasible if its benefits outweigh its costs within a reasonable time.</p>\n<p><b>Criterion 3: Operational feasibility.</b> This asks whether the staff will accept and use the solution in their daily work. A system that slows down serving at the counter, or that staff find difficult, will be bypassed and will fail, even if it is technically excellent.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> First, identifying more than one solution allows the solutions to be compared, so that the optimal, best-value solution is chosen rather than the first idea. Second, it provides a fall-back: if the chosen solution becomes impossible, for example because the vendor stops supporting the product or funds are reduced, a second evaluated alternative is ready.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> One positive impact is that staff save time and work under less pressure. Stock is deducted automatically when each item is scanned, so staff no longer spend the last hour of the day updating cards and counting shelves, and can spend more time advising patients.</p>\n<p>A second positive impact is more accurate information for decisions. The pharmacist receives an accurate reorder report and expiry alerts, so the right medicines are ordered on time, customers are not turned away and patients receive better service.</p>",
    "teacherTip": "<p><b>How marks are lost.</b> Three solutions that are really one (three brands of the same software) earn one set of marks. In (b), criteria must be named and explained: \"cost\" alone earns 1 mark; \"economic feasibility: whether the benefits outweigh development and running costs\" earns 2. In (d), keep the focus on users: staff time, accuracy, less stress, better service.</p>",
