    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> The canteen at Seagrape Bay Secondary School in Speightstown sells lunch to about 600 students using cash only, and each sale is written by hand on a tally sheet. This has resulted in long lunchtime queues, cash shortages at the end of the day, food waste and inaccurate sales reports for the school's bursar.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One cause of the problem is that every sale is processed manually in cash. The cashier must count change and write each item on the tally sheet, which takes about a minute per student, so queues build up and some students give up and go hungry. Handling cash by hand also leads to counting errors and shortages that cannot be traced.</p>\n<p>A second cause is that the canteen keeps no reliable record of what sells each day. Because tally sheets are incomplete and are not totalled by item, the manager guesses how much food to prepare. This leads to popular items such as johnny cakes selling out early while other dishes are thrown away.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Another step is evaluating the possible solutions and selecting the most feasible one. This involves comparing each solution against criteria such as technical, economic and operational feasibility, so that the solution that best fits the canteen's budget, equipment and staff is chosen for implementation.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(i)</b> <b>Interview.</b> This is the most appropriate instrument because there are only five managers, so I can meet each one face to face, ask open questions about the limitations of the manual system and ask follow-up questions to probe and clarify their answers.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(ii)</b> <b>Questionnaire.</b> This is the most appropriate instrument because 100 users can answer the same 15 closed questions at the same time, and their answers produce quantitative data that can be tallied and analysed quickly in a spreadsheet.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(iii)</b> <b>Observation.</b> This is the most appropriate instrument because watching the cashier and students during lunch shows how the system is actually used day to day, including problems that users may not mention or may not notice.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> <b>Interview:</b> one limitation is that interviews are time-consuming to arrange, conduct and write up, and the managers' answers may be biased, for example if they avoid criticising procedures they introduced.</p>\n<p><b>Questionnaire:</b> one limitation is a low response rate, since many users may not return the questionnaire, and closed questions may be misinterpreted with no chance for me to clarify, so the data may be incomplete or inaccurate.</p>\n<p><b>Observation:</b> one limitation is the Hawthorne effect: the cashier may work faster or more carefully because she knows she is being watched, so the behaviour observed is not typical. Observation also cannot show what users think about the system.</p>",
    "teacherTip": "<p><b>How marks are lost in Question 1.</b> In (a), candidates describe the organisation but never say what goes wrong or its effect. In (b), they list symptoms (\"files are lost\", \"reports are late\") instead of causes, or give two causes with no explanation: \"discuss\" needs the cause AND how it produces the problem. In (c), repeating \"define the problem\" earns nothing. In (d), the scenarios are fixed by CXC: five managers = interview, 100 users and quantitative data = questionnaire, day-to-day interaction = observation. A bare instrument name earns only 1 of 2 marks; the reason must use the scenario's numbers or purpose. In (e), give one limitation for EACH instrument, not three limitations of one.</p>",
