    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Solution 1: A prepaid student meal card with a commercial canteen point-of-sale package.</b> The school would purchase an off-the-shelf cashless canteen package. Each student receives a barcode ID card linked to a prepaid account that parents top up at the bursary or online. At lunch the cashier scans the card and taps the items on a touch screen, and the cost is deducted. This removes cash from the queue, speeds up each sale and records every item sold, which addresses both causes.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2: A custom online pre-ordering web app.</b> A local developer would build a web app where students or parents order and pay for lunch before 10 a.m. The kitchen receives a printed list of orders and students collect labelled meals. This reduces the queue and lets the kitchen prepare exactly what has been ordered, reducing food waste.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3: A spreadsheet-based sales system on a laptop.</b> The canteen would use a laptop with a Microsoft Excel sales template with buttons for each menu item. Cash is still taken, but each sale is entered in the spreadsheet, which totals sales and cash expected per day and produces a weekly chart of item sales for planning. This improves records and cash control at low cost, although payment is still in cash.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Solution 1 (prepaid card POS).</b> <i>Technical:</i> feasible; it needs a touch-screen POS terminal, a barcode scanner and a card printer, all standard equipment that the vendor installs and supports. <i>Economic:</i> moderately feasible; the package and hardware cost about BBD$8,000 plus an annual fee, but this is offset by eliminating cash shortages and reducing waste. <i>Operational:</i> highly feasible; scanning a card is faster than counting change, and students and parents in Barbados are used to card payments.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2 (custom pre-ordering app).</b> <i>Technical:</i> partly feasible; it depends on a developer and on students having Internet access in the morning, and online payments require a merchant account. <i>Economic:</i> less feasible; custom development and hosting are expensive for one canteen, and there is ongoing maintenance. <i>Operational:</i> weak; students often decide what to eat at lunch time and may forget to pre-order, so a cash queue would remain.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3 (spreadsheet system).</b> <i>Technical:</i> highly feasible; the school already owns laptops and Microsoft 365, and the IT teacher can build the template. <i>Economic:</i> highly feasible; it costs almost nothing. <i>Operational:</i> partly feasible; staff are familiar with Excel, but entering each sale while handling cash may slow the queue further.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Identifying several solutions lets the school compare their costs, benefits and risks, so that it chooses the optimal solution rather than the first idea. It also means a second evaluated solution is available if the preferred one cannot be implemented.</p>",
    "teacherTip": "<p><b>How marks are lost in Question 2.</b> The commonest loss is three \"solutions\" that are really one (buy a computer, buy a printer, buy software). Each solution must be a different IT approach, described and linked to a cause from Question 1. Listing earns identification marks only; \"discuss\" needs how it works and why it helps. In (b), feasibility must be judged for EACH solution and must name <b>technical</b>, <b>economic</b> and <b>operational</b> feasibility; a sentence such as \"this is feasible because it is cheap\" earns 1 mark at most. In (c), say comparison leads to the optimal choice and gives a fall-back.</p>",
