    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Solution 1: Subscribe to a cloud-based dive booking system (SaaS).</b> The shop would subscribe to an online booking service designed for dive operators. Customers book and pay a deposit through a booking button on the shop's website and Facebook page, and staff log in from the counter PC or a tablet to add phone and walk-in bookings. Every booking goes into one live calendar that stops selling seats once a boat is full, which removes double-bookings and links each deposit to its booking.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2: A custom booking module added to the shop's website.</b> A local web developer would build a booking form and database for the existing website, connected to a shared online calendar. It would be designed exactly around the shop's boats, dive sites and instructor schedules, and would email confirmations to customers.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3: Commercial off-the-shelf reservation software installed on the shop PC.</b> The shop would purchase a desktop reservation package and install it on the counter PC, with staff entering all bookings received by phone, email and WhatsApp. This replaces the paper diary with one central electronic record, but customers would still have to contact the shop to book.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Solution 1.</b> <i>Technical:</i> feasible; it needs only a browser and Internet access, which the shop has through its fibre connection with a mobile data back-up, and the vendor maintains the software. <i>Economic:</i> feasible; the monthly subscription of about US$100 is small compared with the deposits lost through double-bookings, and no server is needed. <i>Operational:</i> highly feasible; overseas customers are used to booking online, and staff need only a short training session.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2.</b> <i>Technical:</i> partly feasible; it depends on finding a skilled developer and on securing online payments. <i>Economic:</i> less feasible; development may cost several thousand dollars plus ongoing maintenance. <i>Operational:</i> feasible once finished, because it is built around the shop's own procedures, but development and testing would take months.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3.</b> <i>Technical:</i> feasible; it runs on the existing PC. <i>Economic:</i> feasible; a one-off licence is affordable. <i>Operational:</i> weak; staff on the boats cannot see or update bookings while away from the PC, and customers still cannot book themselves, so the root cause of multiple channels remains.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Identifying several solutions before choosing allows the owner to compare costs, benefits and risks and select the optimal solution. It also leaves a tested alternative available if the first choice cannot be implemented.</p>",
    "teacherTip": "<p><b>How marks are lost in Question 2.</b> The commonest loss is three \"solutions\" that are really one (buy a computer, buy a printer, buy software). Each solution must be a different IT approach, described and linked to a cause from Question 1. Listing earns identification marks only; \"discuss\" needs how it works and why it helps. In (b), feasibility must be judged for EACH solution and must name <b>technical</b>, <b>economic</b> and <b>operational</b> feasibility; a sentence such as \"this is feasible because it is cheap\" earns 1 mark at most. In (c), say comparison leads to the optimal choice and gives a fall-back.</p>",
