    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> The most feasible solution is Solution 1, subscribing to the cloud-based dive booking system, because it is the most affordable and solves both causes of the problem.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Strategy 1: An online booking button on the website and Facebook page.</b> This is justified because most customers are overseas visitors who plan before they travel; letting them book directly removes most phone and WhatsApp bookings, which was the main cause of the diary being out of date.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 2: Secure online deposits linked to each booking.</b> Card deposits are taken by the booking service's secure payment gateway, so the shop does not store card details. Each booking shows whether it is paid, solving the second cause and reducing no-shows.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 3: One live calendar with seat limits per boat and instructor.</b> The system stops selling once a boat is full, so double-booking becomes technically impossible. Staff on the boat can check the calendar on a tablet, which the paper diary never allowed.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 4: Staff training, data migration and back-up procedures.</b> All staff will receive a two-hour training session from the vendor's online tutorials, and future bookings in the paper diary will be entered by the receptionist before the go-live date. The vendor backs up data daily and access is protected by individual passwords, so the system is reliable and secure. The subscription can be increased if the shop adds a second boat, so the solution can grow with the business.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(i)</b> I would select Solution 2, the custom booking module on the shop's website.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(ii)</b> This alternative is better than the desktop reservation software because it still allows customers in other countries to book and pay online, which removes the root cause of bookings arriving through many channels. It is designed around the shop's exact boats and dive sites, and staff can reach it from any device with Internet access, whereas the desktop package is tied to one PC.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(iii)</b> One disadvantage is that the custom module takes much longer to develop and test than subscribing to a ready-made service, and the shop depends on one developer for fixes. To compensate, the shop could use a shared Google Calendar for all staff as an interim measure, require the developer to deliver the system in phases, and include in the contract full documentation and ownership of the source code so another developer can maintain it.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> Word processing software, such as Microsoft Word.</p>",
