    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> The Windwardside Community Clinic on Saba books patient appointments in a paper appointment book kept at the reception desk. This has resulted in double-booked appointments, patients waiting up to two hours, missed appointments that are never followed up and visiting specialists who fly in from Sint Maarten finding half-empty clinic days.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One cause is that appointments are recorded manually in a single paper book. Only the receptionist can see or change it, so when nurses book follow-up visits by phone they write them on notes that are later copied into the book. Copying errors and lost notes lead to two patients being given the same time slot.</p>\n<p>A second cause is that there is no system for reminding patients of their appointments. The clinic does not record mobile numbers in a usable way and staff have no time to phone every patient, so many patients, especially the elderly, forget their appointments. This leads to wasted slots, particularly on days when a visiting specialist is on the island.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Another step in the problem-solving process is analysing the problem by gathering data. This involves using tools such as interviews, questionnaires and observation to collect facts about how the current system works and what users need, so that the real causes are confirmed before solutions are designed.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(i)</b> <b>Interview.</b> With only five managers, I can hold a face-to-face interview with each one, ask open questions about the limitations of the manual system and use follow-up questions to probe for detail and clarify their answers.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(ii)</b> <b>Questionnaire.</b> A questionnaire is most appropriate because 15 identical closed questions can be given to all 100 users quickly and cheaply, and the answers form quantitative data that can be totalled and graphed.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(iii)</b> <b>Observation.</b> Observation is most appropriate because it lets me watch the receptionist and nurses at work over several days and see how the system is really used, rather than relying on what they say they do.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> <b>Interview:</b> one limitation is that it is time-consuming, since each interview must be scheduled around the managers' duties and then transcribed, and the interviewer may unintentionally bias answers through leading questions.</p>\n<p><b>Questionnaire:</b> one limitation is a low response rate: some of the 100 users may not complete or return it, and because it uses closed questions, I cannot probe or clarify an answer that has been misinterpreted.</p>\n<p><b>Observation:</b> one limitation is that staff may change their behaviour while being watched (the Hawthorne effect), so I may not see the usual shortcuts and errors. Observing patients at reception also raises privacy concerns that limit what I can record.</p>",
    "teacherTip": "<p><b>How marks are lost in Question 1.</b> In (a), candidates describe the organisation but never say what goes wrong or its effect. In (b), they list symptoms (\"files are lost\", \"reports are late\") instead of causes, or give two causes with no explanation: \"discuss\" needs the cause AND how it produces the problem. In (c), repeating \"define the problem\" earns nothing. In (d), the scenarios are fixed by CXC: five managers = interview, 100 users and quantitative data = questionnaire, day-to-day interaction = observation. A bare instrument name earns only 1 of 2 marks; the reason must use the scenario's numbers or purpose. In (e), give one limitation for EACH instrument, not three limitations of one.</p>",
