    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Solution 1: A commercial off-the-shelf clinic management package on a local server.</b> The clinic would purchase a clinic management package and install it on a small server in the clinic, networked to computers at reception and in the consulting rooms. Appointments, patient contact details and visit history are stored in one database, so every staff member sees the same live diary and double-bookings are prevented. The package can print daily patient lists and send SMS reminders through a local SMS service.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2: A cloud-based appointment scheduling service (SaaS).</b> The clinic would subscribe to an online scheduling service accessed through a web browser. Staff book appointments online from any computer, patients receive automatic SMS and email reminders, and visiting specialists in Sint Maarten can view their Saba clinic list before they fly in.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3: A custom-built appointments database.</b> A developer would build a database designed around the clinic's own procedures, with forms for booking, a daily list for each doctor and a reminder list that the receptionist uses to telephone patients the day before. This replaces the paper book but relies on staff making reminder calls.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Solution 1 (commercial package, local server).</b> <i>Technical:</i> feasible; it runs on standard PCs and a small server and does not depend on the Internet, which is important on Saba where connections can be slow and are sometimes interrupted after storms. <i>Economic:</i> moderately feasible; the server and licence cost about US$6,000, but this is a one-off cost with a modest annual support fee. <i>Operational:</i> feasible; the vendor provides training and the screens are designed for receptionists, so staff should accept it.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2 (cloud scheduling).</b> <i>Technical:</i> partly feasible; it requires a reliable Internet connection at all times, and if the link fails reception cannot see the diary. <i>Economic:</i> feasible; there is no server to buy, only a monthly subscription. <i>Operational:</i> feasible; it is simple to use, but patient data is stored with an overseas provider, so the clinic must check that this meets health privacy requirements.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3 (custom database).</b> <i>Technical:</i> partly feasible; there is no developer on Saba, so development and support would be done remotely or by flying a developer in. <i>Economic:</i> less feasible; custom development is costly for a small clinic. <i>Operational:</i> partly feasible; it fits the clinic's procedures, but staff still have to phone patients, which they say they have no time to do.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Identifying several solutions allows the clinic to compare them against the same criteria and choose the optimal one, reducing the risk of a costly mistake. It also provides an alternative if the first choice cannot be implemented.</p>",
    "teacherTip": "<p><b>How marks are lost in Question 2.</b> The commonest loss is three \"solutions\" that are really one (buy a computer, buy a printer, buy software). Each solution must be a different IT approach, described and linked to a cause from Question 1. Listing earns identification marks only; \"discuss\" needs how it works and why it helps. In (b), feasibility must be judged for EACH solution and must name <b>technical</b>, <b>economic</b> and <b>operational</b> feasibility; a sentence such as \"this is feasible because it is cheap\" earns 1 mark at most. In (c), say comparison leads to the optimal choice and gives a fall-back.</p>",
