    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> The most feasible solution is Solution 1: the commercial off-the-shelf clinic management package on a local server.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Strategy 1: Purchasing a tested commercial package.</b> This is justified because it is reliable and already used by other clinics, and it can be installed in weeks rather than the months needed for custom development. The vendor provides updates and remote technical support, which matters on an island with no local developers.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 2: Installing it on a local server.</b> Because the data is stored inside the clinic, the diary keeps working when the Internet link is down, which makes the solution technically suitable for Saba. Patient records stay on premises, which helps the clinic protect confidential health data.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 3: Networking reception and consulting rooms to one database.</b> Nurses and doctors can book follow-up visits directly, so appointments no longer pass through paper notes. This removes the first cause of the problem, double-booking caused by copying errors.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 4: Automatic SMS reminders, training, security and back-up.</b> Reminders sent the day before tackle the second cause, missed appointments, without extra staff time. Staff will receive two days of vendor training before go-live, and each user has a password with access limited to their role. A nightly back-up to an encrypted external drive, stored off-site, protects the data in a hurricane. The one-off cost is affordable through the clinic's equipment budget, and more users can be added later.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(i)</b> I would select Solution 2, the cloud-based appointment scheduling service.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(ii)</b> This alternative is better than the custom database because it can be set up within days with no developer, which Saba does not have, and because it sends automatic reminders, solving the missed-appointment cause that the custom database leaves to staff. Its monthly subscription is affordable and requires no server, and visiting specialists can view their lists from Sint Maarten.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(iii)</b> One disadvantage is that, unlike the local server package, the cloud service depends completely on the Internet connection; if the link fails, reception cannot see who is booked. To compensate, the clinic could install a 4G mobile data router as a back-up connection and print the next day's appointment list every afternoon, so the clinic can continue to see patients during an outage.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> The most appropriate type of application software is presentation software, such as Microsoft PowerPoint. It lets me present each stage of the problem-solving process on clear slides, with charts of the questionnaire results and a diagram of the new appointment process, which helps the class follow and remember the main points.</p>",
