    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> Old Road Fishermen's Co-operative in St. Kitts records the weight and type of fish landed by its 45 members in handwritten notebooks at the landing site. This has resulted in disputes over payments to fishers, lost records when notebooks get wet or go missing, and late, inaccurate catch reports for the co-operative's board.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One cause is that catch data is written by hand at a busy, wet landing site. When several boats land at once, the clerk rushes, figures are written unclearly or in the wrong column, and pages are damaged by water. This leads to wrong weights being used to calculate payments.</p>\n<p>A second cause is that the notebook data is copied manually into a ledger at the office before payments and reports are prepared. Copying the same data twice introduces further errors, and it takes the clerk about two days at the end of each month. As a result, fishers are paid late and the board does not have up-to-date catch figures.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Another step in the problem-solving process is implementing the chosen solution. This involves installing the hardware and software, converting existing data, training users and changing over from the old system to the new one, so that the solution is actually put to work.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(i)</b> <b>Interview.</b> An interview is most appropriate because there are only five managers, so I can speak to each one in depth, ask follow-up questions and clarify exactly which limitations of the manual system affect them.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(ii)</b> <b>Questionnaire.</b> A questionnaire is most appropriate because the same 15 questions can be answered by all 100 users, producing quantitative data that is easy to collect and analyse statistically.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(iii)</b> <b>Observation.</b> Observation is most appropriate because I can watch the clerk recording catches at the landing site over several mornings and see the actual process and where mistakes happen, instead of relying on what users say.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> <b>Interview:</b> one limitation is that it is time-consuming to schedule and conduct each interview, and the answers may be biased because managers may not want to admit weaknesses in procedures they are responsible for.</p>\n<p><b>Questionnaire:</b> one limitation is that some users may not return it, giving a low response rate, and those who do may misinterpret a question, which I cannot clarify because I am not present.</p>\n<p><b>Observation:</b> one limitation is that the clerk may behave differently because she knows she is being observed (the Hawthorne effect). Observation is also time-consuming, as I may need to attend many landings before seeing the busiest conditions.</p>",
    "teacherTip": "<p><b>How marks are lost in Question 1.</b> In (a), candidates describe the organisation but never say what goes wrong or its effect. In (b), they list symptoms (\"files are lost\", \"reports are late\") instead of causes, or give two causes with no explanation: \"discuss\" needs the cause AND how it produces the problem. In (c), repeating \"define the problem\" earns nothing. In (d), the scenarios are fixed by CXC: five managers = interview, 100 users and quantitative data = questionnaire, day-to-day interaction = observation. A bare instrument name earns only 1 of 2 marks; the reason must use the scenario's numbers or purpose. In (e), give one limitation for EACH instrument, not three limitations of one.</p>",
