    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Solution 1: Mobile data capture with online forms and a cloud spreadsheet.</b> The clerk would record each landing on a rugged, waterproof tablet using an online form (for example Google Forms) with drop-down lists for boat, fisher and species. Each entry goes straight into a cloud spreadsheet that totals each fisher's catch and payment automatically. This removes both handwriting errors and copying into the ledger.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2: A commercial co-operative accounting and inventory package.</b> The co-operative would purchase an off-the-shelf package installed on the office PC, with modules for members, purchases of fish, sales to hotels and payments. The clerk would still write catches at the landing site but would key them into the package once a day, which then calculates payments and produces board reports.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3: A custom mobile app linked to an electronic scale.</b> A developer would build an app that reads the weight directly from a digital scale by Bluetooth, records the fisher and species, and uploads the data to a database at the office, producing payments and reports.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Solution 1.</b> <i>Technical:</i> feasible; it needs only a tablet with mobile data and free online tools, and the forms can work offline and upload when a signal returns. <i>Economic:</i> highly feasible; a rugged tablet costs about US$500 and the software is free. <i>Operational:</i> feasible; the clerk already uses a smartphone, and drop-down lists are quicker than writing, though she needs training.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2.</b> <i>Technical:</i> feasible; it runs on the existing office PC. <i>Economic:</i> moderately feasible; the licence and annual support cost more than Solution 1. <i>Operational:</i> partly feasible; it improves payments and reports, but catches are still written by hand first, so the first cause of errors remains.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3.</b> <i>Technical:</i> less feasible; Bluetooth scales and a custom app need specialist skills not available locally. <i>Economic:</i> not feasible at present; development costs several thousand dollars. <i>Operational:</i> highly feasible once built, because weights are captured automatically.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Identifying several possible solutions allows the co-operative to compare them and select the optimal one within its budget. It also reduces risk, because an evaluated alternative is ready if the first choice fails.</p>",
