    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> The Punda Car Rentals in Willemstad, Curaçao, tracks its 60 vehicles on a whiteboard and a basic spreadsheet. This has resulted in cars being double-rented, vehicles missing scheduled servicing, and disputes with customers over damage and fuel because check-out records are incomplete.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One cause is that the status of each car is kept on a whiteboard that staff at the airport desk and the town office update separately. Because the two locations do not share one record, a car returned at the airport may still show as rented in town, or a car may be promised to two customers. This leads to double-renting and lost bookings.</p>\n<p>A second cause is that there is no system for recording mileage, service dates and vehicle condition. Check-out sheets are filled in by hand and are often incomplete, so no one is alerted when a car is due for service, and the company cannot prove what damage existed before a rental. As a result, cars break down and damage claims are lost.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Another step is evaluating the possible solutions and selecting the optimal one. In this step each solution is assessed for technical, economic and operational feasibility and compared using criteria such as cost, time and benefits, so that the most feasible solution is chosen before implementation begins.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(i)</b> <b>Interview.</b> This is most appropriate because there are only five managers, so each can be interviewed face to face, and I can ask probing follow-up questions to clarify the limitations they experience with the manual system.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(ii)</b> <b>Questionnaire.</b> This is most appropriate because 15 standard closed questions can be distributed to 100 users at once, producing quantitative data that can be analysed quickly in a spreadsheet.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(iii)</b> <b>Observation.</b> This is most appropriate because watching staff at the rental desk as they check cars in and out shows how the system is actually used day to day, including shortcuts that users would not report.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> <b>Interview:</b> it is time-consuming to arrange and conduct interviews with busy managers, and responses may be biased by the interviewer's questions or by managers defending their own procedures.</p>\n<p><b>Questionnaire:</b> the response rate may be low because some users do not complete it, and closed questions may be misinterpreted, with no chance to clarify or probe the answers.</p>\n<p><b>Observation:</b> staff may behave differently while being observed (the Hawthorne effect), and observation cannot reveal why users act as they do or what they think of the system.</p>",
    "teacherTip": "<p><b>How marks are lost in Question 1.</b> In (a), candidates describe the organisation but never say what goes wrong or its effect. In (b), they list symptoms (\"files are lost\", \"reports are late\") instead of causes, or give two causes with no explanation: \"discuss\" needs the cause AND how it produces the problem. In (c), repeating \"define the problem\" earns nothing. In (d), the scenarios are fixed by CXC: five managers = interview, 100 users and quantitative data = questionnaire, day-to-day interaction = observation. A bare instrument name earns only 1 of 2 marks; the reason must use the scenario's numbers or purpose. In (e), give one limitation for EACH instrument, not three limitations of one.</p>",
