     "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> At the Punda Car Rentals in Willemstad, Curaçao, the staff track 60 cars on a whiteboard, so cars are double-rented and miss their servicing.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One cause is that the airport desk and the town office each update their own whiteboard. This leads to a returned car still showing as rented, so a car can be promised to two customers.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Another step is evaluating the possible solutions and selecting the optimal one. In this step each solution is checked for technical, economic and operational feasibility.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> (i) Interview, because there are only five managers, so I can talk to each one and ask follow-up questions to clarify what they mean. (ii) Questionnaire. (iii) Observation.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> One limitation of a questionnaire is that some people may not return it, and I cannot ask a follow-up question if an answer is unclear.</p>",
     "teacherTip": "<p><b>How marks are lost in Question 1.</b> In (a), candidates describe the organisation but never say what goes wrong or its effect. In (b), they list symptoms (\"files are lost\", \"reports are late\") instead of causes, or give two causes with no explanation: \"discuss\" needs the cause AND how it produces the problem. In (c), repeating \"define the problem\" earns nothing. In (d), the scenarios are fixed by CXC: five managers = interview, 100 users and quantitative data = questionnaire, day-to-day interaction = observation. A bare instrument name earns only 1 of 2 marks; the reason must use the scenario's numbers or purpose. In (e), give one limitation for EACH instrument, not three limitations of one.</p><p><b>Dial-down tip.</b> Use this version with a student who freezes on the full question. Each short part matches one part of the full question, so once the student scores well here, move them back to the full question and ask them to extend each answer with a second point and an explanation.</p>"
