    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Solution 1: A cloud-based commercial car rental management system.</b> The company would subscribe to an off-the-shelf rental management package hosted online. Both the airport desk and the town office would log in to one shared fleet calendar, so every car's status is updated live. The system records mileage and fuel at each check-out, schedules servicing automatically and stores digital inspection photos taken on a tablet, which addresses both causes.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2: GPS trackers with a fleet monitoring dashboard.</b> A GPS tracking unit would be fitted to each car, reporting its location and mileage to an online dashboard. Managers would see where every car is and receive alerts when a car reaches its service mileage. Bookings would still be managed in the existing spreadsheet.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3: A custom web-based reservation and fleet database.</b> A local developer would build a web application with a database of cars, customers and rentals, designed exactly around the company's rates and procedures, with check-out forms and service reminders.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Solution 1.</b> <i>Technical:</i> feasible; it requires only browsers, tablets and Internet access at both locations, and the vendor maintains the software. <i>Economic:</i> feasible; the monthly subscription is predictable and is less than the income lost from a few double-rentals and breakdowns. <i>Operational:</i> highly feasible; it is designed for rental desks, so staff can learn it quickly, and both offices share one record.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2.</b> <i>Technical:</i> feasible; GPS units are standard, but each must be installed in 60 cars. <i>Economic:</i> less feasible; units and monthly data fees for 60 cars are costly. <i>Operational:</i> partly feasible; it solves service tracking but not double-renting, because bookings stay in the spreadsheet.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3.</b> <i>Technical:</i> partly feasible; it depends on a skilled developer and on secure hosting. <i>Economic:</i> less feasible; high development cost and ongoing maintenance. <i>Operational:</i> highly feasible once completed, because it matches the company's own procedures, but it would take months to deliver.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Identifying several possible solutions lets management compare their costs, benefits and risks and choose the optimal solution, and it provides an evaluated fall-back if the first choice cannot go ahead.</p>",
    "teacherTip": "<p><b>How marks are lost in Question 2.</b> The commonest loss is three \"solutions\" that are really one (buy a computer, buy a printer, buy software). Each solution must be a different IT approach, described and linked to a cause from Question 1. Listing earns identification marks only; \"discuss\" needs how it works and why it helps. In (b), feasibility must be judged for EACH solution and must name <b>technical</b>, <b>economic</b> and <b>operational</b> feasibility; a sentence such as \"this is feasible because it is cheap\" earns 1 mark at most. In (c), say comparison leads to the optimal choice and gives a fall-back.</p>",
