    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> The most feasible solution is Solution 1: the cloud-based commercial car rental management system.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Strategy 1: One shared online fleet calendar.</b> Both offices see the same live status for every car, so a car returned at the airport is immediately available in town. This removes the first cause, separate whiteboards, and makes double-renting technically impossible.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 2: Digital check-out with tablet inspection photos.</b> Staff record mileage and fuel and photograph each car at check-out and return. This gives clear evidence of existing damage, so disputes with customers can be resolved fairly, and the records are complete and legible.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 3: Automatic service scheduling.</b> The system flags cars due for service by date or mileage and blocks them from being rented, which reduces breakdowns and keeps the fleet reliable and safe.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 4: Subscription, training and security.</b> A monthly subscription avoids buying a server and spreads the cost, making it economically feasible. Staff at both offices will receive vendor training before go-live, and the old spreadsheet data will be imported. Each user has a password with role-based access, the vendor backs up data daily, and the subscription can grow as the fleet grows.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(i)</b> I would select Solution 3, the custom web-based reservation and fleet database.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(ii)</b> This alternative is better than GPS tracking because it addresses both causes of the problem: it provides a single shared booking record that prevents double-renting, and it records mileage, condition and service dates. GPS tracking only tackles servicing and location. The custom system also fits Punda's own rates and procedures exactly and can be accessed from both offices.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(iii)</b> One disadvantage is that a custom system takes several months to develop and test, so problems continue longer than with a ready-made subscription, and the company depends on one developer. To compensate, the company could use a shared online spreadsheet between the two offices as a temporary booking record, require the system to be delivered in phases with bookings first, and include documentation and source-code ownership in the developer's contract.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> The most appropriate type of software is presentation software, such as Microsoft PowerPoint, because slides allow me to present each stage of the problem-solving process with bullet points, charts of my questionnaire results and screenshots of the proposed system, which makes the presentation clear for the class.</p>",
