    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> The Hopewell Community Library in Orange Walk, Belize, records book loans on paper cards kept in a box at the front desk. This has resulted in overdue books not being followed up, about 300 books lost in two years, and staff being unable to tell members whether a book is available without searching the shelves and the card box.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One cause is that loans are recorded manually on index cards filed by book title. To find which books are overdue, a librarian must go through every card by hand, which is never done during busy periods. As a result, overdue books are not chased and many are never returned.</p>\n<p>A second cause is that the library has no catalogue linked to the loan records. The book list is an old printed register that does not show whether a book is on the shelf or on loan, so members and staff cannot check availability. This leads to wasted time and frustrated members who stop using the library.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Another step in the problem-solving process is reviewing and monitoring the solution after it is implemented. This involves checking whether the new system meets its objectives, for example whether overdue books and losses have fallen, gathering feedback from users, and making adjustments or maintenance changes so that the solution continues to work well.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(i)</b> <b>Interview.</b> With only five managers, an interview with each one lets me ask open questions about the limitations of the manual system and follow up with probing questions to clarify and obtain detailed answers.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(ii)</b> <b>Questionnaire.</b> A questionnaire is the best choice because the same 15 closed questions can be given to all 100 users quickly and cheaply, producing quantitative data that is easy to tally and analyse.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(iii)</b> <b>Observation.</b> Observation is most appropriate because watching staff lend and receive books at the desk shows how the system is actually used each day, including delays and errors users may not mention.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> <b>Interview:</b> one limitation which should be considered is that interviews are time-consuming to arrange and write up, and answers can be biased by the way the interviewer phrases questions.</p>\n<p><b>Questionnaire:</b> one limitation is a low response rate, since some users will not complete it, and questions may be misunderstood with no opportunity for the researcher to clarify them.</p>\n<p><b>Observation:</b> one limitation is the Hawthorne effect, where staff change their behaviour because they know they are being observed, so what is seen may not be typical. It also cannot show users' opinions.</p>",
    "teacherTip": "<p><b>How marks are lost in Question 1.</b> In (a), candidates describe the organisation but never say what goes wrong or its effect. In (b), they list symptoms (\"files are lost\", \"reports are late\") instead of causes, or give two causes with no explanation: \"discuss\" needs the cause AND how it produces the problem. In (c), repeating \"define the problem\" earns nothing. In (d), the scenarios are fixed by CXC: five managers = interview, 100 users and quantitative data = questionnaire, day-to-day interaction = observation. A bare instrument name earns only 1 of 2 marks; the reason must use the scenario's numbers or purpose. In (e), give one limitation for EACH instrument, not three limitations of one.</p>",
