    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Solution 1: An open-source integrated library system with barcode scanning.</b> The library would install a free, open-source library management system (such as Koha) on a PC at the desk. Each book and membership card receives a barcode, and loans and returns are scanned. The system shows each book's status, lists overdue items automatically and can email reminders, addressing both causes.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2: A cloud-based library management subscription.</b> The library would subscribe to an online library service accessed through a web browser. It provides the same features (barcode loans, catalogue, overdue lists) plus an online catalogue members can search from home, with the vendor hosting and maintaining the system.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3: A custom Microsoft Access database.</b> A volunteer developer would build a database with tables for books, members and loans, with forms for lending and returning and a query that lists overdue loans. It would replace the card box but would not offer an online catalogue.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Solution 1.</b> <i>Technical feasibility:</i> feasible; it runs on a standard PC with a barcode scanner, but installation needs some technical skill, which the library can obtain from a local IT volunteer. <i>Economic (financial) feasibility:</i> highly feasible; the software is free, and only barcode labels, a scanner and a PC upgrade (about BZ$1,800) are needed. <i>Operational feasibility:</i> feasible; scanning is quick and staff can learn it in a short training session.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2.</b> <i>Technical:</i> feasible; it needs only a browser and Internet access, and the vendor handles maintenance. <i>Economic:</i> partly feasible; the annual subscription is a continuing cost for a library that depends on donations. <i>Operational:</i> highly feasible; it is easy to use and members can search the catalogue online.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3.</b> <i>Technical:</i> partly feasible; it depends on one volunteer's skill, and support may disappear if the volunteer leaves. <i>Economic:</i> feasible; Access is already available, so costs are low. <i>Operational:</i> partly feasible; the forms are basic and there are no automatic reminders, so staff must still chase overdue books manually.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Identifying several solutions allows the library to compare their costs, benefits and risks so that the optimal solution is selected, and it means an alternative is already evaluated if the first choice cannot proceed.</p>",
    "teacherTip": "<p><b>How marks are lost in Question 2.</b> The commonest loss is three \"solutions\" that are really one (buy a computer, buy a printer, buy software). Each solution must be a different IT approach, described and linked to a cause from Question 1. Listing earns identification marks only; \"discuss\" needs how it works and why it helps. In (b), feasibility must be judged for EACH solution and must name <b>technical</b>, <b>economic</b> and <b>operational</b> feasibility; a sentence such as \"this is feasible because it is cheap\" earns 1 mark at most. In (c), say comparison leads to the optimal choice and gives a fall-back.</p>",
