    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> The most feasible solution is Solution 1: the open-source integrated library system with barcode scanning.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Strategy 1: Using free, open-source library software.</b> This is justified because there is no licence or subscription cost, which suits a library funded by donations. The software is widely used by libraries, so it is reliable, and it is regularly updated by its user community.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 2: Barcoding every book and membership card.</b> Scanning removes handwritten cards, the first cause of the problem. Each loan is recorded accurately in seconds, and the system knows exactly who has each book.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 3: Automatic overdue lists and email reminders.</b> The system produces a daily overdue list and emails members when books are due, so overdue books are chased without extra staff time, reducing losses. Its catalogue shows whether each book is available, solving the second cause.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 4: Training, data entry, security and back-up.</b> Two volunteers will be trained to install and run the system, and books will be barcoded and entered section by section over the summer holiday. Staff log in with individual passwords, and the database is backed up weekly to an external drive kept off-site. The system can later be placed online so members can search from home, so it supports future growth.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(i)</b> I would select Solution 2, the cloud-based library management subscription.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(ii)</b> This alternative solution is better than the custom Access database because it provides all the needed features immediately, including automatic overdue reminders and a live catalogue, whereas the Access database still leaves overdue books to be chased by hand. The vendor maintains and backs up the system, so the library does not depend on one volunteer's skills, and it needs no server.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(iii)</b> One disadvantage of the cloud subscription, compared with the open-source system, is the continuing annual fee, which a donation-funded library may struggle to pay every year. To compensate, the library could seek sponsorship from a local business in return for acknowledgement on the online catalogue, introduce a small annual membership fee, and choose the vendor's smallest plan, upgrading only when the collection grows.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> The most appropriate type of application software is presentation software, such as Microsoft PowerPoint or Google Slides, because it allows me to present my entire problem-solving process to the class as a sequence of slides with key points, charts of my findings and images of the proposed system, which is clear and engaging for an audience.</p>",
