    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> The customers and dispatch staff of QuickDrop Couriers in Chaguanas, Trinidad and Tobago (who) cannot find out where a parcel is or whether it has been delivered, because parcels are logged by hand in a book and drivers record deliveries on paper slips that return to the office only at the end of the day (how). If this is not rectified, parcels will continue to be lost, customers will keep phoning the office for updates, and the company will lose business customers to competitors that offer online tracking (result).</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One cause is that parcels are recorded manually in a logbook with no unique tracking number. Staff search the book line by line when a customer calls, and parcels with similar names are confused, which leads to lost and misdelivered parcels.</p>\n<p>A second cause is that drivers have no way of updating delivery status while on the road. Proof of delivery is a paper slip handed in at night, so the office cannot confirm deliveries during the day, and slips that are lost leave no record that a parcel was delivered.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> <b>Interviews:</b> an interview is a planned face-to-face (or video) conversation in which the researcher asks the owner, dispatcher or drivers prepared questions, follows up with further questions and records the answers. It gathers detailed information about how the current system works and its problems.</p>\n<p><b>Observation:</b> observation involves the researcher watching users carry out their normal work, for example a dispatcher logging parcels and a driver completing deliveries, and recording what happens on a checklist. It shows the actual process, delays and errors as they occur.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> I have chosen the interview.</p>\n<p><b>Advantage 1: Answers can be clarified and probed.</b> Unlike a survey, where a respondent's vague or misunderstood answer cannot be checked, in an interview I can ask follow-up questions on the spot. For example, if the dispatcher says \"parcels go missing\", I can ask when and how, which validates the data immediately and saves time later.</p>\n<p><b>Advantage 2: It produces more detailed, in-depth information.</b> A survey usually uses closed questions that limit answers to fixed options. An interview lets the owner explain the whole process, including problems I had not thought of, so I gain a deeper understanding of the causes of lost parcels.</p>\n<p><b>Disadvantage: It is more time-consuming and costly for large numbers of people.</b> A survey can be sent to all 25 drivers and many customers at once and analysed quickly, but each interview must be scheduled, conducted and written up individually. This takes much more time, so only a few people can be interviewed, and their views may not represent everyone.</p>",
    "teacherTip": "<p><b>How marks are lost.</b> In (a), a problem statement missing the \"what if not rectified\" element loses a mark. In (c), surveys are excluded, so naming questionnaires earns nothing. In (d), the comparison with surveys is the point: \"interviews are detailed\" earns 1; \"unlike a survey, an interview lets me ask follow-up questions, so vague answers can be clarified on the spot\" earns 3.</p>",
