    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Solution 1: A cloud-based courier management system with a driver smartphone app.</b> QuickDrop would subscribe to an online courier management service. Every parcel receives a barcode tracking number when it is logged; drivers scan parcels and capture a signature and photo on a smartphone app at delivery; and customers track parcels on a web page. This removes both the logbook and the paper slips.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2: A commercial off-the-shelf parcel tracking package on an office server with handheld scanners.</b> The company would purchase a package installed on its own server. Parcels are barcoded and scanned in and out at the office, and drivers use handheld scanners that are synchronised when they return. This creates an accurate electronic record but updates delivery status only at the end of the route.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3: A custom-built web tracking system.</b> A local developer would build a web database and a simple mobile web page for drivers, designed around QuickDrop's own routes and customers, with SMS updates to customers when parcels are delivered.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> 1. Time needed to develop and implement the solution. 2. Human impact, such as possible redundancies of clerical staff. 3. Infrastructure required, such as equipment, network and Internet access. 4. Staffing with highly skilled employees to run and support the system. 5. Training required for staff to use the system.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> For Solution 1, the cloud-based system:</p>\n<p><b>Time:</b> because it is a ready-made subscription, it can be in use within about two weeks, so the company starts reducing lost parcels almost immediately, unlike a custom system that would take months.</p>\n<p><b>Infrastructure:</b> each of the 25 drivers needs a smartphone with a mobile data plan, and the office needs reliable Internet. This means an extra monthly data cost, and deliveries in areas with weak signal will only update when the phone reconnects.</p>\n<p><b>Training:</b> drivers and dispatchers must be trained to scan parcels and capture signatures. This means sessions must be held early in the morning before routes start, so deliveries may be slightly slower during the first week while staff gain confidence.</p>",
