    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> I would use a weighted scoring matrix. First, I list the criteria that matter, such as cost, time to implement, ease of use and security, and give each a weight according to its importance (for example, cost 30%, time 25%, ease of use 25%, security 20%). Then each solution is scored out of 10 on each criterion, each score is multiplied by its weight, and the results are added. For example, the cloud system scored 8 × 0.30 for cost, 9 × 0.25 for time, 8 × 0.25 for ease of use and 7 × 0.20 for security, a total of 8.05, which was higher than the other two solutions, so it was selected as most feasible.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> This statement is partly true. At a given point in time one factor, often cost, can decide the choice: after a hurricane or a severe flood, for example, many small Caribbean businesses have very limited budgets, so the cheapest workable solution is often selected even if it is not the best long-term option. However, cost should not be the only factor. A cheap solution that staff will not use, or that is insecure, may cost more in the long term through lost parcels and customers. Also, the prevailing factor changes: when a company has funds, time to implement or long-term benefits may matter more. Therefore, the optimal solution depends on the circumstances at that time, but a good decision balances several factors rather than cost alone.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(i)</b> The most appropriate application is a word processor such as Microsoft Word. A report is a text-based document, and a word processor is designed for writing and editing long text, with features such as heading styles, tables, spelling and grammar checking and page layout tools that produce a professional, well-formatted report.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(ii)</b> <b>Feature 1: Automatic table of contents.</b> By applying heading styles to each section (problem statement, analysis, solutions, recommendation), I can insert a table of contents after the cover page. It lists every section with its page number and updates automatically, so readers can find and navigate to any section quickly.</p>\n<p><b>Feature 2: Headers and footers with page numbers.</b> A header containing the report title and company name, and a footer with automatic page numbers, appear on every page after the cover. This gives the report a consistent, professional look and makes pages easy to reference in discussion.</p>\n<p><b>Feature 3: Section breaks and a cover page.</b> A cover page with the title, author and date is placed at the start, and section breaks separate the main parts of the report, so each major section starts on a new page and can have its own layout, for example a landscape page for the feasibility comparison table. This organises the report into clear, structured parts.</p>",
