     "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> First I would list the criteria, such as cost, time and security. Then I would score each solution against each criterion and compare the totals. Finally I would choose the solution with the highest score.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> Cost is often the deciding factor because a small business may have a limited budget, as many do after a hurricane or a severe flood. However, security also matters because a cheap system that loses customer data costs more in the end.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> I would use word processing software such as Microsoft Word because a report is a long text document.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> Three features are a table of contents, headers and footers with page numbers, and heading styles.</p>",
     "teacherTip": "<p><b>How marks are lost.</b> In (a), a description with no example caps you at 2–3 marks. In (b), agreeing in one line (\"yes, cost is most important\") is not a discussion. In (c)(i), this is a REPORT, so a word processor is right; presentation software earns nothing here. In (c)(ii), naming bold, italic and underline is weak; choose features that organise the report (table of contents, headers/footers, section breaks) and say where they are placed.</p><p><b>Dial-down tip.</b> Use this version with a student who freezes on the full question. Each short part matches one part of the full question, so once the student scores well here, move them back to the full question and ask them to extend each answer with a second point and an explanation.</p>"
