    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> Hummingbird Ridge High School keeps student attendance, grades, discipline records and teacher reports manually in paper registers, while office staff use separate spreadsheets to prepare reports. This has resulted in duplicated student records, inaccurate attendance reports, misplaced records, report cards reaching parents late and excessive staff time spent preparing reports.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One cause is the continued reliance on paper registers. Teachers record attendance and grades by hand, and the registers must be collected and read by office staff, which increases transcription errors and the risk of documents being misplaced. This leads to inaccurate attendance reports and lost records.</p>\n<p>A second cause is the lack of an integrated information system. Each office clerk keeps a separate spreadsheet, so the same student is entered several times with slightly different details. This causes duplicate records and inconsistent reports, and staff spend extra time combining data, which delays report cards.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Another stage is requirements analysis (analysing the current system). This involves gathering detailed information from users such as teachers, office staff and the principal to determine the functions, inputs, outputs and constraints the new system must have, so that the solution meets the school's real needs.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(i)</b> <b>Interview.</b> The principal is one person who needs to explain procedures in detail, so a face-to-face interview allows the analyst to ask probing questions and clarify each step of the reporting procedures.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(ii)</b> <b>Questionnaire.</b> With 900 students, interviews are impractical; an online questionnaire with mainly closed questions can be sent to all students at once, because it is quick, cheap and produces data that is easy to analyse.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(iii)</b> <b>Observation.</b> Watching teachers during lessons shows exactly how attendance is recorded in practice, how long it takes and where errors occur, which teachers might not describe accurately.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> <b>Interview:</b> it is time-consuming, and the principal's answers may be biased towards how procedures are supposed to work rather than how they actually work.</p>\n<p><b>Questionnaire:</b> the response rate may be low, since many students may not complete it, and some may misinterpret questions or answer carelessly, which cannot be clarified.</p>\n<p><b>Observation:</b> teachers may change their behaviour because they know they are being watched (the Hawthorne effect), and the analyst's presence may disrupt the lesson.</p>",
    "teacherTip": "<p><b>How marks are lost in Question 1.</b> In (a), candidates describe the organisation but never say what goes wrong or its effect. In (b), they list symptoms (\"files are lost\", \"reports are late\") instead of causes, or give two causes with no explanation: \"discuss\" needs the cause AND how it produces the problem. In (c), repeating \"define the problem\" earns nothing. In (d), the scenarios are fixed by CXC: five managers = interview, 100 users and quantitative data = questionnaire, day-to-day interaction = observation. A bare instrument name earns only 1 of 2 marks; the reason must use the scenario's numbers or purpose. In (e), give one limitation for EACH instrument, not three limitations of one.</p>",
